From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable

Mike Price:

>> Hence linear energy of motion is transferred to internal vibrational
>> modes (ie friction is generated) as surfaces slide over eachother.

Derek Zahn:

> A question: Are these vibrational modes and their effects what are
> modeled as phonons?  Is that modeling technique accurate?

Probably not.  Phonons are usually modelled against a repeating lattice
structure, where a phonon is a lattice distortion that propagates like
a wave.  Whether the concept can carry over to more general structures
I doubt (although I don't know for sure).

>> it would not surprise
>> me to find out that these hard-to-calculate subtleties have been
>> ignored in the calculations.
> 
> Mike, I'm surprised at you.  You base your general agreement of
> James's criticisms of Drexler's treatment of VdW forces based on
> your expectations of what D's might have neglected?

Based on my knowledge of the approximations used to construct molecular
models (as of 10 years ago), which (I presume) are the algorithmic input
into the simulator programs that folks like Merkle use.  QM can predict
the distribution of the electron around a proton (a hydrogen atom) and
(to a lesser extent) for a hydrogen molecule.  Beyond that and we're
talking spherical chickens....

> If our lot is to be blind men analyzing an elephant, let's at least
> touch it!

..Heard the joke about a physicist who attempts to model a chicken but
has to assume spherical symmetry before analysis can start? - perhaps
a more apt metaphor for nanotech :-)

> derek

