 I'll have to keep this short and reply at length later. For now let me 
simply say that I've got no problem with believing everything Eric said  
happened actually happened (at some level of Reality or another :) ).
 My experiences with the martial arts, and acting, and troubleshooting 
(this is my technological manifestation of possession by Genius) have 
shown me repeatedly that left brain explanations are useless when 
describing right brain phenomena. 
 I found that "explaining" the phenomena in approved psychological/
physiological/neurological terms made me feel more secure, more rational. 
"I'm note some granola chomping MYSTIC fer chrissake, I'm doing a 
neurological experiment that entails putting myself in a , er, Alpha 
state(!! Yeah, that's the ticket) and ,er, using, uh, *biofeedback* 
babblebabble I achieved a *hyperalert* state........ad nauseum." Then I 
noticed that explaining it got in the way of DOING it. So I stopped trying 
to explain everything and concentrated on -scratch that, *relaxed* into 
doing it.
 What is "it"? Breaking boards/bricks with punches, kicks, palms and 
fingertips. Breaking the third brick down leaving the top two unscathed.
Taking punches, kicks, blows from sticks, shinais, staffs without pain or 
bruising. Being unmoveable. Being unstoppable. Being lifted by pressure 
points for demos. Reacting with speed and precision without conscious 
thought.
 On stage I was able to achieve all the Stanislavsky goals without becoming 
an unprofessional flake. I often achieved that hot state where you are 
acting and delivering all your lines, but are so "into" the moment that you 
have no idea what you are going to say next. You are as surprised as your 
character. Or doing standup when I'd feel the power and drop the routine I 
had written and ad lib. And judging from the audience's reaction I was 
funnier than hell, but once again had no idea what was going to come out of 
my mouth. 

 Tim Robbins wrote that the East offered Light and the West offered Warmth.
Same fire, different aspects. I've sampled the East and learned a lot. Or 
maybe I unlearned a lot. But by and large progress(?) came from solitude.
I'm intrigued with the idea of practicing a Way that involves, and at times 
requires other people. 
 That idea wouldn't have intrigued me years ago, so maybe karate taught me 
something besides dealing out rations of kickass.
 My only worries about getting involved with West Coast pagans is the 
signal-to-flake ratio. But, upon reflection....I work with programmers 
and engineers, am an active Libertarian, hang with karate punks and 
Mensans; what's a few more flakes more or less?
