Subject: NANO: microorganism knobs and levers, gears and wheels

> > It appears that such items as flagella and cillia [...]

In this context it might be useful to distinguish between the locomotive
apparatus of single-celled eukaryotic cells (like _Protozoa_ and _Amoeba_)
from those of prokaryotes (bacteria and cousins).  The former kinds of
cells have linear dimensions typically of 10 microns and up; the latter
start at more like one micron.  Thus as you might expect, prokaryotic
flagella are much simpler than their eukaryotic functional equivalents
(actually, not quite functional equivalents, I think I recall).  If memory
serves (my microbiology text is at home, so it will have to), the prokaryotic
structures really do work in a rather mechanistic way.

                                -- Jay Freeman


