From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable

Taking Ray Cromwell's comments out of order (but not, I hope, out of
context):

> Michael Clive Price writes:
>>I believe some of the paradigms in nanotech are inappropriate.  These
>>are:
>>1)   classical rod-and-stick designs

>   Point #1 sounds reasonable
> Rod-and-stick designs remind me of the Difference Engine.

Me too!  Very nicely put.  Rather than using gears and cogs we had
valves, transistors and then microchips.  That's not to say that a
mechanical rod-logic computer can't work, but I doubt whether they will
ever be built except to stick in a museum of might-have-beens like the
Babage exhibition.  As Ray goes on to describe, nanoelectronics sounds
much more plausible.

>>2)   the nanotech self-replicator assembler

>, point #2 sounds wrong by example. As people
> are fond of mentioning, Nature "already does it."

Nature does it, but will we bother?  Notice that I used the word
inappropriate, not impossible.  I believe the economy provides a better
model for the path to nanotech than analogies with bacteria.  General
self-replicating assemblers are a poor, inefficient way to produce
miracles.  The industrial revolution is a better miracle to examine. 
During it mankind discovered the advantages of mass production, assembly
lines and specialisation (the division of labour).  Nanotechnology, IMO,
is just continuing the trend of miniaturisation (microchip fab plants,
bio- and genetic engineering, STMs, etc.) we see in microtechnology. 
The products of the future will not be made in vats by teeming billions
of replicating nano-assemblers all milling about.  They will be made in
assembly-line factories, financed by the usual investment of capital,
to reap the advantages of mass-production, just as they are now.

There will be no magic point when nanotech 'lifts off' to create the
Singularity (no, _that_ particular miracle happens when the supply of
human labour is replaced by cheaper AIs - ie labour and
entrepreneurship, two of the factors of production, become a subset of
capital, a third, and the economic growth feedback loop tightens,
shifting into higher gear.)

> -- Ray Cromwell

