For some people, that makes the difference between chronic unhappiness and
an O.K. life; for others it makes the difference between O.K. and "WOW!"
(I think I'm in the latter category.)

What the "right" form of initiation is varies from person to person.  Wicca
and shamanism were right for me.  Right for you might be Thelema, or Zen,
or Sufism, or Santeria, or any one of hundreds of other forms of mind-change.

One of the basic skills of a neopagan priest is matching people to the dieties
and ritual forms that will function best for them, whether or not they're
within the priest's favorite tradition.  (You're invited to think of
this as practical psychology or a form of therapeutic analysis if you're
more comfortable with that language.)

I don't know you well enough to make a firm prescription.  Zen, maybe.
That's relatively easy for people starting from an exclusive Western-
intellectual head to grasp, and good for widening one's perceptions to
the point where stuff like the internal logic of mystery religions
begins to make sense.  (No, you wouldn't have to give up your rationality.
I never did.)
