Enough is enough! The rantings about Witchcraft and Paganism on this List
are beyond belief.

My judgment has been far too long in coming (mea culpa). I admit to losing
interest about 6-8 weeks or so ago, after having already written far too
much. I could see no reasonable way to finish my large report (258 KB is
the current size--I'll have it on my PowerBook 170 at the Thursday
Extropian lunch for anyone who wants to see it in its still-not-finished
form) and I could see no real point in putting another hundred hours of
work to finish it properly. I didn't think the "witches" on the List were
going to be swayed, and the blessed silence on the subject I took to mean
no one else was all that interested, either.

I should have at least issued my judgment, which was determined by then,
rather than letting sleeping Pagans lie.

But it seems that Paganism has been reincarnated, reanimated with the
spirit of the Elder Gods and channeled by Eric the Godhead, who has been
scribbling furiously the past few days about his sexual prowess, his poetic
genius, his musical excellence, his unmeasurable IQ,  and how much less
geeky he is than we who live in the dark cave of Rationality.

Enough is enough.

Accordingly, I'm including  the capsule summary part of Chapter 1, "Summary
and Introduction." For reference, here are the major headings,  the
chapters:

Pagan Trial

1. Summary and Introduction
2. The Challenge and My Approach
3. Paganism, Witchcraft, Druidism, Shamanism, and Magick
4. Religion and Belief Systems
5. Truth, Objective Reality, and Belief
6. Prometheus and Gaia: Two Opposing Worldviews
7. Conclusions and Discussion
8. References and Source Materials

And here is the capsule summary, included in Chapter 1, of my judgment in
this matter:


Capsule Summary

In short, I am not out looking for a coven to join.

Paganism, and its specific instantiation Wicca, is just another
polytheistic, superstition-laden, Gaia-centered mystery religion and can
make no plausible claims for special treatment or from exemption from the
tests of rationality and irrationality we apply to any religion, cult, or
set of superstitions.
 
Paganism and Witchcraft are, on balance, irrational and inconsistent with
the rational, science-oriented, technophilic attitude characteristic of the
Extropians list. Can one imagine an article on witchcraft as a serious 
belief system in "Extropy," for example?

The appeal to Pan, the Horned God, Legba, the Goddess, Astarte, whatever
(the union of Pagan deities results in a huge pantheon of every god and
goddess from Babylon to Hawaii to Siberia), even as Jungian archetypes or
psychological aids is no different than any mystical tradition or religion.
The "metaphorical" afterlife, as one example, is not what Extropians seek:
we seek a literal form of immortality, via cryonics and cell repair or
uploading, and not a kind of metaphorical or religious immortality. (For
those who can't distinguish between objective and subjective reality, this
example is instructive.)

While some (but not all) Pagans are themselves rational and not overly
mystical, the same may be said about nearly any irrational belief system,
which does not change the evaluation of the overall belief system,
religion, cult, or whatever. In short, it's just as flaky as New Age
occultism, belief in ghosts, or  engram-encrusted Scientology.

Put bluntly, I find Witchcraft and Paganism to be no more rational a belief
system than faith healing, alchemy, sacrifices to appease the gods, Tarot
cards, parapsychology, casting runes, crystal balls, astrology, Qabalism,
astral projection, Christian spirituality, soothsayers, the Elder Gods, UFO
abductions, consorting with familiars, Islamic devil worship, shaman vision
quests, Rosicrucianism, flying yogis, reading entrails, the Enochian
entities, exorcism, Da Free John, raising the dead, stigmata, or the
channeling of ancient Indian warriors.

All of these belief systems have undeniable attractions to some seekers,
even to scientists. And they may "work" in the sense of auto-suggestion and
faith in general. But they are not real in any sense except that of
delusion and willingness to believe. And they are, in any case, what the
Extropians list is all about (even with a liberal interpretation of
"self-transformation" and "boundless optimism").

Peter Pan, the Easter Bunny, and Santa Claus are equally comforting myths,
and just as believable. (It would not surprise me at all to learn that
there are organizations devoted to these belief systems!)

Further, Paganism cannot claim to be any less irrational than more
traditional religious systems...just another set of gods, another set of
rituals and superstitions. (A  rhetorical ploy by mystics is to claim that
all of these beliefs are valid paths on the journey to enlightenment, that
they are different facets of the same reality, that Seekers take many
routes, blah blah blah.)

Anyone who takes any of this stuff seriously has some fairly serious
delusions. The religious impulse is poorly understood, and may have various
psychological, social, and memetic roots, but the beliefs remain
delusional. Paganism is largely a mixture of faith healing, earth worship,
and Catholic ritual. The distribution of beliefs of Pagans, Witches, and
Druids between these extremes is unknown to me.

In its mildest form, where the social gatherings (Wiccan holidays,
religious festivals) and transformative rituals are emphasized, it
resembles conventional religions like Catholicism or Hinduism, perhaps less
uptight in some ways. In more extreme forms, where the focus is on the
casting of spells, the invocation of spirits, the creation of "faery
circles," the purging of "thought forms," and so forth, it is almost
totally irrational and anti-Extropian. The belief by many Pagans in
reincarnation or in an afterlife (which  some of them call "Summerland") is
completely counter to the  life extension, cryonics, uploading, and related
approaches favored by Extropians. 

(Whether the Extropian-favored approaches are "right" or plausible is not
the issue, only whether apparent Pagan beliefs are consistent with them.
Clearly they are not. If it turns out that Eric Raymond will be playing his
Pan pipes in Summerland while we Extropians sleep forever in cryonic
suspension, so be it--he'll have the last laugh. But I wouldn't bet money
on it.) 

This summarizes my judgment. This view is not "proved" beyond any
doubt--such a matter of religious faith likely cannot be objectively
disproved, although specific predictions or causal explanations can and
have been disputed. I was challenged only to review various books and
whatnot, and then make a judgment. 

And so I have.


